Although many SBUs have been reported, the diversity of the constituent anionic elements of which they are composed is poor because most successful SBUs are constructed using oxygen- or nitrogen-based bridging ligands.7b,8 The use of sulfur atoms instead of oxygen atoms (sulfur-SBUs) has recently been reported to be an attractive option for expanding atomic diversity among SBUs and adjusting properties such as band gaps9 and conductivities.10 For instance, molybdenum− sulfur SBUs have been reported as electrocatalysts,11 and cobalt−sulfur SBUs have been reported to be conducting materials.12 Moreover, sulfur-SBUs in MOFs are stabilized through the construction of frameworks, whereas metal− thiolate complexes are generally unstable in air because of sulfur oxidation.13 Although this strategy, which involves constructing sulfur-SBUs in MOFs, is very useful and promising in terms of creating unique properties, the formation of discrete structures hampers the development of sulfur-SBUs that not only are electrically conducting but also have appropriate band gaps for visible-light absorption. Hence, new sulfur-SBUs with infinitely extended structures and evaluation of their conduction mechanisms continue to be pursued.14
In this work, we focused on trithiocyanuric acid $\left(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{ttc}\right)$ as an organic sulfur ligand. Over the past three decades, some ttcbased coordination polymers with unique functionalities, such as visible-light absorption,15 photocatalysis of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution by Ni-ttc16 and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ evolution by Cd-ttc,17 and electrical conductivities,18 have been reported. Therefore, we hypothesized that MOFs composed of $\mathrm{H}_{3}$ ttc could be promising functional materials with unique photocatalytic and electronic properties. However, the contribution of the metal ions to the functionalities of such MOFs is still unclear because, with the exception of complexes of ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{\mathrm{I}}$ , group I, and group II ions,18,19 the crystal structures of ttc-based coordination polymers have not been determined.20 Exploring the relationships between their crystal structures and unique electrical properties is therefore a desirable objective. We chose to investigate a structure with lead as the metal ion because of the unique electrical properties of PbS nanostuctures.21 In addition, highdimensional MOFs are expected to be formed by various $\mu_{2}{-}S$ and/or $\mu_{3}–S$ bonds because of the high coordination number of lead ions,22 and the strong lead−sulfur coordination bond, generated by a soft Lewis acid−base interaction, should provide robust frameworks with high chemical stability.23 Furthermore, high conductivity is expected because of the large overlap integrals of $\mathrm{Pb}$ ion orbitals. MOFs that include Pb-SSBUs are expected to exhibit novel functionalities, such as unique semiconductor-like electronic structures, because the SBUs are considered to be semiconducting nanostructures. In this study, we successfully determined the crystal structure of a three-dimensionally extended sulfur-SBU-based MOF that we call KGF-1 (Kwansei Gakuin Framework-1). KGF-1 exhibits interesting sorption behavior, a semiconducting band structure, and the ability to photocatalytically split water upon visiblelight absorption.
The crystal structure of as-synthesized $\mathrm{Pb}_{3}\mathrm{ttc}_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (KGF$\mathrm{1{\cdot}H_{2}O}^{\cdot}$ ) reveals that KGF- $\mathrm{1{\cdot}H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ has two crystallographically nonequivalent $\mathrm{Pb}$ atoms (Figure 1a). One Pb atom (Pb1)
possesses eightfold coordination (with two coordinated water molecules), and the other (Pb2) has sevenfold coordination (Figure S1a,b). These high coordination numbers are attributed to the large ionic radius of $\mathrm{Pb}$ .
The key coordination element that forms the polymer structure is sulfur because all of the sulfur atoms bridge $\mathrm{Pb}$ atoms. The sulfur atoms exhibit two coordination modes: S2 is a $\mu_{3}–S$ atom, while S1 and S3 are $\mu_{2}{-}S$ atoms. S2 connects one Pb1 and two Pb2 ions, while S1 connects two $\mathrm{Pb}2$ ions and S3 connects one Pb1 and one $\mathrm{Pb}2$ ion. Thus, the $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ions are linked to each other through the ttc $^{3-}$ ligands to form a threedimensional (3D) structure (Figure ${1}\mathsf{b},\mathsf{\bar{c}}$ ).
KGF- $\mathrm{1{\cdot}H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ has two types of $\mathrm{Pb}$ -N,S-based SBUs, a onedimensional (1D) infinite chain SBU along the $b$ axis and a zero-dimensional (0D) SBU (Figure 1d,e). The 1D-chain SBU is formed through $\mathrm{Pb}2$ atoms with edge-sharing structures along the $c$ axis composed of ttc3 dimers (Figure S1d,e), while the 0D SBU consists of the Pb1 atom. The 0D SBUs are located along the $b$ axis between the 1D SBU chains, and pores are formed as a result of this structural feature. The pores of assynthesized KGF- $\cdot\mathrm{1{\cdot}H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ are filled with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules. The SBUs are also 3D-connected, and hence, four 1D-chain SBUs are connected through a 0D SBU.
To evaluate its electronic and optical properties, KGF-1· $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was subjected to diffuse-reflectance ultraviolet−visible− near-infrared (UV−vis−NIR) spectroscopy in order to evaluate the band gap energy. The spectrum in Figure 3a reveals an absorption maximum at $425~\mathrm{nm}$ , with the band gap determined to be $2.57~\mathrm{eV}$ (Figure 3a inset). The position of this intensity maximum is red-shifted with respect to its position in the spectrum of the ligand (Figure S5), suggesting that the coordination of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ to the $\mathrm{H}_{3}$ ttc ligand is essential for the visible-light absorption properties and smaller band gap of KGF-1. We also performed photoelectron yield spectroscopy (PYS) to determine the absolute energy of the highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) (Figure S6). The PYS results show that the HOMO is located ${\mathfrak{s}}.9{\mathfrak{s}}\ {\mathfrak{e V}}$ below the vacuum level. The band gap and absolute energy levels determined using these spectroscopic techniques are shown in Figure 3b. Moreover, we also used the CASTEP first-principles calculation method24 to calculate the band structure and density of states distribution using the degassed-phase KGF-1 structure without structural optimization. The calculated band gap is $2.19\ \mathrm{{\eV}},$ , and the partial density of states (PDOS), HOMO, and lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) reveal that the valence and conduction bands are dominated by sulfur orbitals and hybrid carbon, nitrogen, sulfur, and lead orbitals, respectively (Figures 3c and S8). The maps for the HOMO and LUMO demonstrate that the HOMO is mainly localized on sulfur atoms while the LUMO is delocalized. Since the calculated molecular orbital reveal that the $\mathrm{Pb}2$ atoms and ttc ligands mainly contribute to the LUMO, the main conduction path most likely spans the hybrid orbitals of Pb2 and ttc. The diffuse-reflectance UV−vis−NIR spectroscopy and PDOS results show that KGF-1 exhibits visible-light absorption due to the high energies of the atomic orbitals of sulfur; hence, charge is transferred from the sulfur orbitals to the hybrid orbitals upon absorption of visible light. The photoconduction properties of ${\mathrm{KGF}}{\cdot}1{\cdot}{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ were evaluated via time-resolved microwave conductivity $(\mathrm{TRMC})^{25}$ experiments (Figure S9). The $\varphi\Sigma\mu_{\mathrm{max}}$ value of $\mathrm{KGF-1\cdotH_{2}O}$ powder was determined to be $7.4\times\overline{{{10}}}^{-5}{\mathrm{~cm}}^{2}{\mathrm{~V}}^{-1}{\mathrm{~}}s^{-1}{\mathrm{~}}$ which is comparable to those of reported conductive $\mathrm{MOFs}^{26}$ and covalent organic frameworks (COFs).27 The transient shows a spontaneous rise within the instrumental response time and a moderate decay with fast $(\tau=0.22~\mu s)^{\mathrm{~~}}$ ) and slow $(\tau=16~\mu s)$ components, which means that charge carriers are generated and migrate. Using femtosecond transient absorption spectroscopy, we also elucidated the mechanism of the initial stage of photoconduction. The results suggested that carriers are generated (Figure S10).
Encouraged by the above characterization data, we examined the photocatalytic activity toward $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution. The KGF-1· $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ framework was maintained during photoirradiation under the catalytic reaction conditions, as confirmed by XRPD, and more than 95 wt $\%$ of the KGF-1 was collected by centrifugation after the photoirradiation (Figure S11). The amount of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolved increased with time upon irradiation with light for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ (Figure 4a). The total amount of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolved was approximately $51\mu\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ over $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ , which corresponds to $0.14\ \mu\mathrm{mol}$ . Several control experiments were performed in the absence of KGF- $\begin{array}{r}{1\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},}\end{array}$ light, or $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{EDTA}$ (sacrificial donor); $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ gas was not detected by GC under any of these conditions. A smaller amount of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ gas was detected in the absence of $\mathrm{K_{2}P t C l_{4}}$ (a precursor of the Pt cocatalyst). These results indicate that KGF- $\boldsymbol{1\cdot H_{2}O}$ can conduct photoexcited electrons because the generated electrons must migrate within KGF-1· $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ to reduce the proton at the adsorbed $\mathrm{Pt}$ on the crystal surface. These results suggest that KGF- $\mathrm{1{\cdot}H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ acts as a photocatalyst for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production, $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{EDTA}$ acts as a sacrificial electron donor, and Pt acts as a cocatalyst to enhance the hydrogen-evolution capability. Accordingly, we propose a possible mechanism for the charge-separation and hydrogenproduction processes observed when KGF- $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{l}}{\cdot}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{O}}$ is irradiated with visible light (Figure 4b). KGF- $\mathrm{1{\cdot}H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ absorbs visible-light photons with energies greater than its band gap to generate electrons and holes. The holes are subsequently quenched by the sacrificial donor, $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{EDTA}$ . The photoexcited electrons migrate and are received by the Pt cocatalyst, leading to reduction of the protons on $\mathrm{Pt}$ to generate molecular $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . The migration is attributed to electron conductivity, which is consistent with the results of the TRMC measurements and the computed LUMO electron density maps.
In conclusion, we successfully synthesized a novel MOF, KGF- $\mathrm{1{\cdot}H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{\cdot}$ , based on $\mathrm{Pb}$ -S-SBUs with 3D structures and determined its crystal structure by single-crystal X-ray diffraction. KGF- $\mathrm{1{\cdot}H_{2}O}$ exhibits selective adsorption behavior and absorbs visible light. This MOF has a semiconductor-like electronic structure, as evidenced by first-principles calculations and TRMC measurements. Because of its appropriately aligned energy levels, KGF- $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{l}}{\cdot}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{O}}$ can be used as a photocatalyst to accelerate the water-splitting hydrogenevolution reaction under visible-light irradiation. We have also evaluated the correlation between its crystal structure and functionalities, including its photocatalytic capabilities, though first-principles calculations. This work provides a new structural design strategy for MOFs based on sulfur atoms as coordinating and bridging species.
